The &quot;RGB&quot; in &quot;getRGBImage&quot; refers to the color space rather than the exact image format, this behaviour is explained in the JavaDoc:

{quote}
Returns the content of the given image as an AWT buffered image with an RGB color space. If a color key mask is provided then an ARGB image is returned instead.
{quote}

The image is either RGB or ARGB. Color key masks are very rare and images use a lot of memory and CPU time, especially alpha channels, so we only use ARGB is we really have to.

EDIT: The bug fix looks good to me, I&apos;ll mark this issue as Fixed.

{quote}
Some algorithms don&apos;t care, which may result in inconsistent ordering across multiple calls
{quote}

I don&apos;t see how this would happen unless the algorithm was randomised. For a given input the output should always be the same, regardless.

But as you say the ordering is not sufficiently defined, so there may be more than one solution. Perhaps we need some more sophisticated rules for determining reading order? Off the top of my head, it seems like [Topological sorting|http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Topological_sorting] may be of relevance.

