[~msahyoun], I&apos;m not sure that anyone was suggesting that PDField should inherit from PDNonTerminalField, as you say that wouldn&apos;t be correct.

Here&apos;s what I had in mind: if we want to preserve the existing notion that a PDField represents an actual field (i.e. a non-terminal field) then we could use a class hierarchy like that below:

{code}
abstract class PDFieldTreeNode
class PDNonTerminalField extends PDFieldTreeNode
class PDField extends PDFieldTreeNode
{code}

And the following constructors:

{code}
protected PDFieldTreeNode()
protected PDFieldTreeNode(PDNonTerminalField parent)

public PDNonTerminalField()
public PDNonTerminalField(PDNonTerminalField parent)

public PDField()
public PDField(PDNonTerminalField parent)
{code}

The PDFieldTreeNode class would expose only the properties which can be inherited, it will also include the field inheritance logic, which will lookup the given key on it&apos;s &quot;parent&quot; when it does not have the value locally. The PDNonTerminalField class would contain little code, and exist mostly just to be a concrete implementation of PDFieldTreeNode. The PDField class will contain just the code for those extra properties supported by terminal fields.

