Indeed it does, though in this case it was actually a problem with the font, some of the charstrings contain &quot;0 0 [15]&quot; but 15 is not a valid charstring command in Type 1 and it&apos;s reserved in Type 2.

I&apos;ve fixed this by ignoring and warning when encountering truly unknown commands in [r1608985|http://svn.apache.org/r1608985].

How about ignoring the translation for all glyphs and using it instead as parameters when drawing the glyphs?
{code}
graphics.drawGlyphVector(glyphs, x, y); 
{code}

The following seems to make the trick, tested on a single file so far, though:
{noformat}
    private void writeFont(final AffineTransform at, final GlyphVector glyphs)
    {
        // Convert from PDF, where glyphs are upright when direction is from
        // bottom to top, to AWT, where this is the other way around
        at.scale(1, -1);
        for(int i=0; i&lt;glyphs.getNumGlyphs(); i++)
        {
            AffineTransform glyphTransform = glyphs.getGlyphTransform(i);
            Point2D glyphPos = glyphs.getGlyphPosition(i);
            AffineTransform applyTransform;
            if(glyphTransform != null || glyphPos.getX() != 0 || glyphPos.getY() != 0) {
                AffineTransform translate = AffineTransform.getTranslateInstance(glyphPos.getX(), glyphPos.getY());
                if(glyphTransform != null)
                {
                    translate.concatenate(glyphTransform);
                }
                translate.preConcatenate(at);
                applyTransform = translate;
                glyphs.setGlyphPosition(i, new Point2D.Float(0, 0));
            }
            else 
            {
                applyTransform = at;
            }
            glyphs.setGlyphTransform(i, applyTransform);
        }
        graphics.drawGlyphVector(glyphs, 0, 0);        
    }
{noformat}

I see. Why not do the same, then - apply the transform to the path instead of the graphics? The following works like magic on your test file. I am just not sure whether it might negatively affect performance and I tested it with this single file only (pattern-shading-2-4-idMatrix.pdf). 

Alternatively, we might either be able to compute the right transformation from the deviceBounds/userBounds in AxialShadingPaint#createContext()  (I am not sure, I just think the information we need might be in there), or use a custom rendering hint key and pass the additional transform along with the rendering hints.

{noformat}
    private void drawGlyph2D(Glyph2D glyph2D, int[] codePoints, AffineTransform at) throws IOException
    {
        graphics.setRenderingHint(RenderingHints.KEY_ANTIALIASING, RenderingHints.VALUE_ANTIALIAS_ON);
        for (int i = 0; i &lt; codePoints.length; i++)
        {
            GeneralPath path = glyph2D.getPathForCharacterCode(codePoints[i]);
            if (path != null)
            {
                if (!at.isIdentity())
                {
                    path = (GeneralPath) path.clone();
                    path.transform(at);
                }
                graphics.fill(path);
            }
        }
    }
{noformat}

Another good news is that the time to render all files is the same, despite the warning in the javadoc of setGlyphTransform().

