I&apos;m not a signature expert but as fas as I can know 
{code}
String signature = new COSString(sign).getHexString();
{code}
produces a string without the enclosing &quot;&lt;&gt;&quot;. So that I can&apos;t see a need for your first patch

IMO the second one is also invalid. If a given signature is written as hex string we need twice as much bytes as in the signature itself. There are 2 addtional bytes needed for the enclosing &quot;&lt;&gt;&quot;. Looks like 
{code}
preferredSize * 2 + 2 
{code}
is just the right thing to do.

Or am I missing something?

{quote}
produces a string without the enclosing &quot;&lt;&gt;&quot;. So that I can&apos;t see a need for your first patch
{quote}

It produces the string wihtout &quot;&lt;&gt;&quot;, but the allocated space in the pdf i.e. {code}signaturePosition[1] - signaturePosition[0]{code}  includes &quot;&lt;&gt;&quot;, so you must subtract 2 bytes when comparing it with the signature length (in bytes). The original code subtracted only one byte (&quot;&lt;&quot;), making it possible to overwrite &quot;&gt;&quot; and screw up the pdf.

{quote}
IMO the second one is also invalid.
{quote}

You are wrong again, what do you think {code}setContents(new byte[100]){code} does? It certainly does not allocate 100 byte in the PDF. It allocates just enough space to store 100 bytes encoded as a hexadecimal string i.e. 202 bytes in the PDF. The original code would allocate 406 bytes by doing x2 + 2 unnecessarily.

I looked at this today. I now understand how we experience this problem.

We have a class, Divider, which has a number of methods. We&apos;ll start at method a() which accepts a ByteArrayInputStream that holds a PDF. a() calls splitter() handing it this ByteArrayInputStream and expecting a list of PDDocuments back.

class Divider {

public List&lt;byte[]&gt; a(ByteArrayInputStream pdfBytes) {

List&lt;PDDocument&gt; split = splitter(pdfBytes);
List&lt;byte[]&gt; retVal = new ArrayList&lt;byte[]&gt;();
for (PDDocument p : split) {
ByteArrayOutputStream os = new ByteArrayOutputStream();
p.save(os);
retVal.add(os.toByteArray());
}
return retVal;
}

public List&lt;PDDocument&gt; splitter(ByteArrayInputStream masterPdf) {
List&lt;PDDocument&gt; retVal = new ArrayList&lt;PDDocument&gt;();
PDDocument doc = PDDocument.load(masterPdf);
List&lt;PDPage&gt; pages = doc.getDocumentCatalog().getAllPages();

// Iterate over the pages and import each page into new PDDocuments added to retVal
return retVal;
}
}

Because splitter internally creates new PDDocument and performs importPage() referencing the individual pages from the master document, the master document falls of of scope the moment the splitter&apos;s work is done. Not unreasonable.

The trouble is that importPage passes the new PDPage a reference to the original&apos;s scratchFile. So the moment the GC clears up the master document having returned to a() the scratchFile is closed, causing a()&apos;s saving to crash. All blindingly obvious when you realise the scratchFile is being copied inside the importPage routine which most people might expect would perform a clean clone.

That hopefully concludes things. We can of course re-work our code to avoid the bug but it would be sensible to make importPage perform a proper clone at some point.

Ah, I see. I guess the addPage is the culprit here. Try to adapt the splitter code (copied from Splitter#processNextPage)

